            "A child's circulation is small and her reserves are small, but her compensation is powerful: losing fluid or blood, she raises her heart rate and clamps down her peripheral vessels and <strong>keeps her blood pressure normal until a quarter to a third of her volume is gone</strong> — then collapses abruptly. So shock in a child is recognised by the signs of compensation — <strong>tachycardia</strong> out of proportion to fever or fear; <strong>cool, pale or mottled hands and feet</strong> with the coldness creeping up the limbs; a <strong>capillary refill time over two seconds</strong> (press the sternum or a nail bed for five seconds, release, count); <strong>weak peripheral pulses</strong> with a brachial or femoral pulse that is still felt; <strong>reduced urine output</strong>; <strong>tachypnoea</strong> from acidosis; and <strong>a change in mental state</strong> — the irritable, anxious child who becomes drowsy and quiet. Hypotension is <strong>decompensated</strong> shock and precedes arrest by minutes. The causes in Ghana are, in order, <strong>hypovolaemia</strong> from diarrhoea, haemorrhage and burns; <strong>sepsis</strong> (meningococcal, pneumococcal, Gram-negative, severe malaria); <strong>severe anaemia</strong> (malaria, sickle cell sequestration, hookworm); and less often <strong>cardiogenic</strong> (myocarditis, congenital heart disease, rheumatic heart disease), <strong>anaphylactic</strong> and <strong>neurogenic</strong>.",
