          alt: "Estimating burn surface area in a child: the rule of nines is modified because a child's head is proportionally larger and the legs smaller — in the infant the head is 18 per cent, each leg 14 per cent, each arm 9, front of trunk 18, back 18; the head loses about 1 per cent and the legs gain each year until adult proportions at about ten years; the Lund and Browder chart is more accurate; the palmar rule — the child's own palm with fingers is about 1 per cent of her surface — is used for scattered burns. Depth: superficial (red, painful, no blisters), superficial partial (blisters, moist, pink, very painful), deep partial (drier, blotchy, less painful), full thickness (white, leathery or charred, painless). Referral criteria for a child: over 10 per cent total body surface area, any full-thickness burn, burns of the face, hands, feet, perineum or over joints, circumferential burns, inhalation injury, electrical or chemical burns, and any burn where non-accidental injury is suspected.",
          caption: "A child's head is a fifth of her surface in infancy and her legs a seventh each; the child's own palm is one per cent. Over ten per cent, any full-thickness burn, a special area, a circumferential burn, a burned airway or a suspicious pattern — refer.",
