          alt: "Antidotes nurses are expected to know, paired with the poison: paracetamol with N-acetylcysteine; opioids with naloxone; benzodiazepines with flumazenil; organophosphates with atropine (and pralidoxime); iron with desferrioxamine; warfarin with vitamin K; heparin with protamine; digoxin with digoxin-specific antibody; methanol or ethylene glycol with fomepizole or ethanol; carbon monoxide with 100 per cent oxygen; cyanide with hydroxocobalamin; methaemoglobinaemia with methylene blue; snake bite with antivenom; beta-blockers with glucagon; local anaesthetic toxicity with lipid emulsion; anticholinergic poisoning with physostigmine; and activated charcoal 1 g per kg within one hour for most oral poisons but not hydrocarbons, caustics, iron, lithium or alcohols. Supportive care — airway, breathing, circulation, glucose, seizures, temperature — matters more than most antidotes.",
          caption: "Charcoal within the hour for most tablets, never for kerosene, caustics or iron; N-acetylcysteine for paracetamol, desferrioxamine for iron, atropine for organophosphates, naloxone for opioids, antivenom for the envenomed child — and airway, breathing, circulation and glucose before any of them.",
